/*!
    \page vscodeext-tutorials-build-run-qt-example.html
    \previouspage vscodeext-tutorials.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-how-tos.html

    \ingroup vscodeext-tutorials

    \title Tutorial: Build and run Qt examples

    This tutorial illustrates how you can open Qt examples in VS Code.
    To test that your \l{Get and Install Qt with \QOI}{\QOI} installation is
    successful, open an existing example application project, and then build and
    run it.

    \section1 Before you start

    Before you start, you have to:

    \list
        \li \l {Installation}{Install \QVSC}
        \li \l {Register Qt installations}
    \endlist

    \section1 Open a Qt example

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette}, and then select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Open Qt examples}.
            \image {vscodeext-qt-examples.webp} {List of Qt examples}
            If you cannot see any examples, check that the list of
            \uicontrol {Qt versions} is not empty.
        \li Select an example in the list of examples.

            You can also use tags to filter examples.
        \li Select \uicontrol {New project} to create a new project based on the
            example project.
            \image {vscodeext-qt-examples-new-project.webp} {Creating a new project based on a Qt example}
        \li In \uicontrol {Name}, enter a name for the project.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Open in new window}.
            \image {vscodeext-qt-examples-open-in-new-window.webp} {Opening the example project in a new window}
        \li Select a kit for for building and running the project.
        \li Select
            \inlineimage {run_project.png} {Launch the selected target in the terminal window}
            on the status bar.
        \li Select the executable as the launch target to build and run the
            project.
    \endlist

    \sa {Installation}, {Register Qt installations}, {Tutorials},
    {Create Qt projects}
*/
